You sign in to the class server from a crowded train. You have a
half-read report open and a shell waiting in the right directory.
At the next station the wireless link vanishes. When you reconnect,
will you see the same shell? A new SSH login generally gives you a new
shell. If you want to return to the previous work area, arrange for
it to have a life of its own \emph{before} the link fails.

\begin{objectives}
\item Distinguish a remote login, a running task, and a detachable workspace.
\item Practice attaching and detaching with a private local tmux server.
\item Decide when a saved file or a managed job is a better safeguard.
\end{objectives}

\section{The connection isn't the workspace}
\index{remote session}\index{terminal multiplexer}
The remote shell from Chapter~\ref{ch:38} lives on the server, but it
normally uses a login terminal associated with the SSH connection.
When the link goes down, that terminal may close and its shell and
jobs may end. Exactly what happens depends on the shell, program,
and service policy. An interrupted connection doesn't tell you that
every process stopped; a remote command may already have finished or
some processes may continue independently. Conversely, setting a
command running in the background with \texttt{\&} doesn't establish
that it will survive logout. Background jobs in Chapter~\ref{ch:32}
described who controls a current terminal, not a policy for keeping
processes across sessions.

\index{tmux@\texttt{tmux}}\index{detaching}
The optional tool \FirstUse{tmux} is a terminal multiplexer. It runs
its own server process on the machine where you invoke it. That server
maintains a collection of named sessions, with windows and panes in
which shells and other programs run. A pane is a space that displays
one terminal; this chapter uses just one pane. The client is the
program showing that session in the window you're looking at.
Outside of tmux, you can have a regular shell and no tmux client
attached. Inside, the client connects back to its server and displays
one of the sessions. Detach the client and the session can keep its
pane and shell. A later client can attach to the same session.
In Figure~\ref{fig:40-location}, notice where the pane shell is:
closing a window on your laptop doesn't move it back to the laptop.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-40/location.tikz}
\caption{A remote tmux session lives where the tmux server runs.}\label{fig:40-location}
\VisualDescription{The laptop terminal reaches a server through SSH. The tmux workspace and its pane shell are both on that server.}
\end{figure}

The word ``server'' does double duty: both the class machine and
the tmux process may be called a server. In this chapter the
\emph{host} is the machine and the \emph{tmux server} is the process
on it. If you start tmux on your laptop, it keeps a workspace on your
laptop. Running SSH inside a local pane doesn't make a remote login
independent of the connection. To keep remote panes, start tmux
inside the verified remote login. If you later reconnect to a different
host behind the same service name, the process may not be there.
Ask the provider before relying on tmux on a shared service.

This distinction is easier to remember if you ask which process
would still be there after disconnecting. The text you saw in a
terminal window was a view of a process, not the process itself.
With tmux in the middle, the client provides the view and the
server maintains the pane terminal. With an ordinary login there's
no independent tmux server to provide that terminal. An occasional
disconnect doesn't warrant adding a multiplexer to everything.
If you only need to read a finished report, a saved report is a
simpler thing to carry from one session to the next.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.45in}Y}
\toprule
Action or event & What to expect \\
\midrule
Detach a client & The pane shell and its tasks may continue; your underlying shell becomes visible again. \\
Exit the only pane shell & The pane closes, and with no more windows the session normally disappears. \\
Lose an SSH link & A remote tmux client can disconnect while its session survives on the host; ordinary SSH shells have no such workspace to reattach to. \\
Reboot the host & The running tmux server and unsaved in-memory state do not normally survive. Files you saved may remain. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Start a workspace you can safely close}\label{sec:40-lab}
Use a real interactive terminal with a shell accepting the \texttt{sh}
syntax. Do not start this exercise from inside another multiplexer;
its shortcuts could go to the wrong client. \cmd|command -v| shows
the shell's interpretation of a command name. \cmd|tmux -V|
prints the version without starting a server. On Linux, macOS,
FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD, use the installed manual and the
package or base tools available on your system. tmux is not specified
by POSIX. If it isn't installed, read the steps as an illustrated
walkthrough; don't install software on a remote machine without
the operator's permission.

A short disposable exercise is also a way to tell whether tmux
works well with your terminal settings before committing much work
to it. Check whether you can recognize the status line and send
the prefix reliably. A screen reader or terminal not configured for
full-screen applications may make it harder to locate the cursor or
distinguish the shell prompt from the status line. The session list
and a known value printed in the pane are checks that don't depend
on appearance. If display fails, stop and use a normal shell to
continue with saved files, rather than adjusting an unfamiliar
production account to make the screen match a printed example.

\begin{terminal}
command -v tmux
tmux -V
\end{terminal}

Make a private workplace. \texttt{-d} asks \cmd|mktemp| for a
new directory; save its path under \texttt{sessionlab}. If setup
fails, stop. Commands below use the variable from this original
shell, not from a different window or a later login.

\begin{terminal}
sessionlab=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$sessionlab"
pwd
\end{terminal}

The first tmux invocation sets up a separate server. A \FirstUse{socket}
is a local endpoint through which a tmux client can talk to the
tmux server. \texttt{-S} gives its pathname. This socket is named
\texttt{lab.sock} in your new directory, not a default socket that
might belong to existing work. For the initial start, \texttt{-f /dev/null}
chooses an empty configuration instead of loading a site or personal
tmux configuration. \texttt{new-session} starts the session;
\texttt{-s lesson} gives it a name, and the final \texttt{sh} is
the pane program to launch. The specific \texttt{sh} found in your
environment may have local shell settings independent of tmux.

\begin{terminal}
tmux -S "$sessionlab/lab.sock" -f /dev/null new-session -s lesson sh
\end{terminal}

You should now see a shell prompt with the tmux status line, often at
the bottom of the window. The status line normally includes a session
name and window information; don't require it to have a certain
color. If your terminal reports an unknown terminal type or no
terminal, use the steps in Chapter~\ref{ch:36} to describe the error.
Do not randomly assign \texttt{TERM} values to force it: that
environment variable describes capabilities of the terminal, and
misidentifying them can make output or keys harder to use.

At the prompt inside tmux, check \cmd|pwd|. It should be the
practice directory because you started tmux there. If not, don't
write files: detach and check the setup. The next command puts a
short string in a shell variable without exporting it or saving it
to disk. Use it to see that the original pane shell is still running
after you return. This is not where to keep a real document.

\begin{terminal}[title={In the tmux pane shell}]
pwd
marker=still-here
printf '%s\n' "$marker"
\end{terminal}

\section{Leave without closing, then return}
The default tmux \FirstUse{prefix} is \KeyChord{Ctrl}{b}: press
\texttt{b} while holding Control, release them, then press
\Key{d} on its own. The second key tells tmux to detach.
Don't type the words \texttt{Ctrl-b d} at a shell prompt.
Your earlier prompt should return, possibly with a notice that
the client detached. By contrast, \cmd|exit| inside the pane
would close its shell. These are two different operations.
Figure~\ref{fig:40-detach} shows attach and detach as inverse operations
on the client, with the workspace unchanged beneath them.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-40/detach.tikz}
\caption{Detach changes the client state, not the pane's shell.}\label{fig:40-detach}
\VisualDescription{An attached client becomes detached while the session and its pane remain. Attaching again restores the display.}
\end{figure}

Back at the outer shell, continue to specify the private socket.
\texttt{list-sessions} asks that server about its sessions rather
than about all programs on the machine. Look for a line starting
with \texttt{lesson:}; the rest varies by version and environment.
\texttt{attach-session} opens a client view of the named
session and \texttt{-t} selects the target. On current tmux
versions \texttt{=lesson} requests an exact session name.
No effect on your normal tmux server is intended.

\begin{terminal}[title={Back at the shell outside tmux}]
tmux -S "$sessionlab/lab.sock" list-sessions
tmux -S "$sessionlab/lab.sock" attach-session -t '=lesson'
\end{terminal}

In the pane, the same variable still has its earlier value. An
empty line would not demonstrate that the variable survived:
recheck whether you are really looking at the original pane shell.
This calculation is local even if you are reading about remote use.

\begin{terminal}[title={Inside the reattached pane}]
printf '%s\n' "$marker"
\end{terminal}

Here is a compact map of the steps. A real remote workflow differs
chiefly in where the steps happen: connect with SSH first; detach;
leave the remote shell if finished for now; later sign back in to
the same host and attach to the same session under the same account.
The lesson's private socket is an isolation device for practice.
On a real server follow local instructions about where to put session
state. In particular, the temporary area might be cleaned automatically
and a provider's session limits might stop idle jobs.

If you leave by detaching first, you can see the remote shell
return and choose whether to close the SSH login. Deliberately
leaving is a better rehearsal than pulling a network cable.
For real work, tell yourself the host, account, and session name
before signing out. If the provider has separate front-end and
worker hosts, ask where a user session belongs. Remembering just
the session name is not enough if the same account has different
workspaces on different machines. A session name that describes
the work, such as \texttt{minutes-review}, is more useful than
one you will mistake for the previous week's task.

The saved name isn't a password. On a properly configured multiuser
machine, access to your tmux socket is restricted separately from
the name you assign to a session. Anyone already allowed to attach
to that workspace can potentially see what's on its screen and
type into the pane. If your provider supports a shared session,
learn the approved access controls before contributing anything
sensitive. With more than one client attached, text or a cursor
can move because someone else is using it; don't assume the
terminal has started to misread your keys. Our isolated socket
is specifically for practice as one user and isn't intended as a
connection-sharing mechanism.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.55in}Y}
\toprule
Context & Operation \\
\midrule
Outer shell on the host & A tmux command can create, list, or attach to sessions on the selected socket. \\
Terminal attached to tmux & Default prefix followed by \texttt{d} detaches this viewing client. \\
Shell in a pane & Shell commands run on the host. The pane shell's variables remain local to that shell. \\
Remote login outside tmux & \texttt{exit} closes that login; it doesn't normally end a previously detached tmux session. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{A workspace is not a save button}
\Brian{If I leave my editor open in tmux, am I backed up?}
\Stephen{You can return to the editor if the session is still alive.}
\Brian{That's not what I asked. What if the server restarts?}
\Stephen{Then the editor's unsaved changes may be gone. I should distinguish returning from restoring.}
\Brian{So saving protects me from losing the session, but not from losing the disk.}
\Stephen{Right. A separate recoverable copy addresses a different problem again.}
\Brian{Then I'll save before detaching instead of trusting the status line.}
\Stephen{And restore a test copy when you make a backup.}
\DialogueConclusion{Detaching preserves a session, not a backup.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{When something is missing}
Suppose the SSH connection returns, but the session doesn't appear.
First check machine and account, as in Chapter~\ref{ch:38}. Check
the spelling of the session and, if you used a private socket, the
socket path. The local and remote shells can both run the same tmux
command, but they won't see a shared list just because the words
are identical. Also check whether you deliberately closed the last
pane shell rather than detaching. If a long operation was running,
find out whether it finished before starting a duplicate. For
example, a file conversion may have produced a complete file already,
or left a partial one that should not be delivered.
Figure~\ref{fig:40-recover} makes the check of existing work the
next step when no session appears.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-40/recover.tikz}
\caption{Recover by checking the host, account, session, and saved files.}\label{fig:40-recover}
\VisualDescription{After reconnecting, check host and account first, then check the session. If absent, inspect saved outputs before restarting any work.}
\end{figure}

A detachable workspace is convenient when you need to see or interact
with a program after returning. If the job must finish unattended,
investigate the managed job facilities your provider offers. These may
impose time limits, capture logs, or give the job a recoverable
identifier. If what matters is the document itself, write changes to
disk at sensible points and make a separate backup (Chapter~\ref{ch:41}).
A live shell isn't a durable record, and its scrollback can contain
filenames, credentials accidentally displayed, or other private
output. Don't share a session, change socket permissions, or let another
user attach just to show them a screenshot. Send a redacted diagnostic
instead, through a channel approved for the work.

The time to decide whether an operation can be resumed is before
you start it. Suppose you're editing a short note. Saving to a
new draft and reviewing it on your next login may be all you
need. Suppose you're converting a large recording. Decide where
the completed output belongs, how you'll recognize it, and where
error messages will go. Choose an unused output name, so an
interrupted conversion cannot erase your only finished derivative.
Suppose instead that your job is to review a long interactive
report from the middle. Leaving a multiplexer session may make
sense, but write down where you stopped in the report as a
fallback. ``I can recover by going to page 28'' is a plan
whether or not the tmux session returns.

If you close the pane in the practice exercise, don't start a
new one automatically to hide the result. There should no longer
be a variable to recover. In real work the previous shell might
also have had several environment settings that a newly started
shell doesn't know about. A short, private note about the current
directory, which tool you used, and what output you expected can
make it easier to restart safely. Don't record secrets as part
of that note. It is meant to let you resume the task, not turn
the entire terminal state into a log.

\section{Close the practice session deliberately}
Do the first two exercises before the final cleanup. At the pane prompt
type \cmd|exit|. It closes the one pane shell. The original outer shell
returns; it still has the variable with the practice directory.
If you explicitly detach instead, attach to \texttt{lesson} again
before exiting. Do not close other tmux sessions to find your way
back to this one.

\begin{terminal}[title={In the practice pane only}]
exit
\end{terminal}

In the outer shell check that the practice tmux session no longer
appears. With no remaining sessions the server ordinarily exits;
the socket path might disappear or remain as a stale entry. A
message saying no server is running or the socket is missing is
then expected. If it still reports a session, don't remove the
socket while the server is live.

\begin{terminal}[title={Outside tmux}]
tmux -S "$sessionlab/lab.sock" list-sessions
pwd
ls -a "$sessionlab"
\end{terminal}

If the directory is empty apart from the directory entries
\texttt{.} and \texttt{..}, use \cmd|cd| and \cmd|rmdir "$sessionlab"|.
For a leftover socket, stop and check that no practice server is
running before removing only that socket with \cmd|rm -i|.
Unexpected files are not a reason to use recursive removal.

\begin{exercise}{Find the original shell again}\label{ex:40-reattach}
In your practice pane change the marker to \texttt{returned}. Detach,
list the practice sessions and attach again. Check that the new value
appears; leave the lab running for the second exercise.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|marker=returned| at the pane prompt. Press the prefix
then \Key{d}, use the same private socket path with \texttt{list-sessions}
and \texttt{attach-session}, and run \cmd|printf '%s\n' "$marker"|
in the pane. Expect \texttt{returned}, not a new prompt's default value.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Compare the outer and inner shell}\label{ex:40-context}
Set \texttt{marker=outside} in the outer shell after detaching. Inspect
its value there, reattach, and inspect the value inside. Explain why these
don't overwrite one another and keep the lab for the final exercise.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
The outer shell prints \texttt{outside} with
\cmd|printf '%s\n' "$marker"|. The pane should still print its previous
value. The two shell processes don't share ordinary shell variables
and reattaching doesn't create a new pane process.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Check that the practice session ended}\label{ex:40-cleanup}
Exit from the only practice pane, then check the private server
from the outer shell. Remove the practice directory only after the
session is gone and you know what the directory contains.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
At the pane prompt use \cmd|exit|, then follow the listing and
cleanup steps above. Expect no remaining practice session; the
client may report a missing server socket. Once the directory is
empty, \cmd|cd| and \cmd|rmdir "$sessionlab"| finish the lab.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Start remote workspaces on the host where the work must continue.
\item Detach to leave a workspace running; exit a pane shell to close it.
\item Save your work and plan for a host restart, not just a broken link.
\end{takeaways}

